---
title: Multi-Language Support
description: 'Nx works with any language or toolchain. Plugins infer projects, tasks, and dependencies from the configuration files your tools already use.'
sidebar:
  order: 10
filter: 'type:Features'
---

Nx works with any language or toolchain.
The task runner, caching, and [affected](/docs/features/ci-features/affected) commands all operate on the project graph, and the graph is plain data.
Plugins produce that data by reading configuration files that already exist in your repository, so adding support for a new language means teaching Nx to read the files that language already uses.

## Projects and tasks are inferred

Nx doesn't need a plugin to run tasks for another language.
Any directory with a [`project.json`](/docs/reference/project-configuration) file is an Nx project, and its targets can run `pytest` or `cargo build` with the same caching and orchestration as any JS task.

A plugin removes the duplicate configuration.
Your toolchain already describes each project in its own files, so maintaining a `project.json` next to every `pyproject.toml` or `Cargo.toml` means writing the same information twice.
A language plugin declares which files mark a project, usually the toolchain's manifest: `pyproject.toml` for Python, `go.mod` for Go, `Cargo.toml` for Rust, `build.gradle` for Gradle.
For every match, the plugin creates a project and its tasks, with caching, inputs, and outputs configured once in the plugin instead of per project.
This is the same [inferred tasks](/docs/concepts/mental-model#inferred-tasks) mechanism the JavaScript plugins use to read `vite.config.ts` or `jest.config.ts`.

Enabling a language plugin is one entry in `nx.json`:

```jsonc
// nx.json
{
  "plugins": ["@nx/gradle"],
}
```

From there, `nx test <project-name>` runs the toolchain's own test command, `nx affected` picks up changes to any project, and cache hits restore outputs no matter what language produced them.
Run `nx show project <project-name>` to see everything a plugin inferred for a project.

## Dependencies come from your toolchain

Plugins also tell Nx how projects relate by reading the dependency information your toolchain already wrote down, like the `dependencies` entries in a `pyproject.toml` or the project references in a Gradle build.
Those edges are what make `nx affected`, task ordering, and the [graph visualization](/docs/features/explore-graph) accurate across languages, and they connect cross-language boundaries too, such as a deploy task that depends on both a JS frontend and a Java backend.

## Use an existing plugin

Nx maintains first-party plugins for [Gradle](/docs/technologies/java/gradle/introduction), [Maven](/docs/technologies/java/maven/introduction), and [.NET](/docs/technologies/dotnet/introduction).
Community plugins cover more ecosystems, such as [@nxlv/python](https://github.com/lucasvieirasilva/nx-plugins/tree/main/packages/nx-python), [@nx-go/nx-go](https://github.com/nx-go/nx-go), and [@monodon/rust](https://github.com/cammisuli/monodon/tree/main/packages/rust).
Check the [plugin registry](/docs/plugin-registry) before writing your own.

## Build your own plugin

If your toolchain isn't covered, a plugin that creates projects, tasks, and dependencies is a few hundred lines of TypeScript built on two functions, `createNodes` and `createDependencies`.
Follow [add language support to Nx](/docs/kb/add-language-support) for a complete walkthrough, using Python with uv as the worked example, that you can adapt to any language.
